//! core/net/server — being a server.
//!
//! `core/net/http` is the client half: `http.send(ctx, request)` makes one
//! request and answers one response. This is the other half — a program that
//! *is* a server rather than one that talks to one — and they are two modules
//! because they are two authorities. `Net` in a context means the program talks
//! to servers; `Listen` means it accepts connections, and `Sockets` means it
//! can push on one somebody else accepted.
//!
//! **The same two types.** `Request` and `Response` are `core/effect`'s, the
//! ones `core/net/http` re-exports: there is one shape for an HTTP message in
//! this language, not one per direction, so a handler here answers with the
//! same `Response` a client reads, and builds it with the same
//! `http.text` / `http.status` / `withHeader`.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Listen, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/net/http" import * as http;
//! from "core/net/server" import * as server;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Listen: host.listen,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     server
//!         .serve(ctx, server.Server {
//!             port: 3000,
//!             onRequest: fn(c, request) => {
//!                 match (request.path()) {
//!                     "/ping" => http.text(c, "pong"),
//!                     _ => http.status(404),
//!                 }
//!             },
//!         })
//!         .mapErr(server.errorText)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Every knob is a field, and an omitted one is `.None`
//!
//! `Server` carries the whole configuration — address, protocols, limits and
//! deadlines — and every field but `port` and `onRequest` is an `Option`. A
//! literal may leave an `Option` field out and the checker fills in `.None`
//! (SPEC 5.6), which is why the example above is three lines long.
//!
//! **`.None` is not "the default"; it is the absence of a choice, and the
//! runtime is what turns the absence into a default.** That distinction is the
//! library convention this module asks readers to hold, and it is worth the
//! sentence because elision makes it easy to miss: writing nothing does not
//! commit this module to a number, so nothing here documents "the default
//! address is `127.0.0.1`" as a promise. The acceptor picks, and a toolchain
//! whose runtime picks differently is not breaking this module's contract. A
//! program that wants a number pinned writes the number.
//!
//! `bind` is where the `.None`s are rendered into what `effect Listen` calls
//! *unset*, and this is the whole of the encoding:
//!
//! | Field | `.None` crosses as | And the acceptor today |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `address` | `""` | listens on `127.0.0.1` |
//! | `protocols` | no `.Speak` in the plan | speaks HTTP/1.1 |
//! | `tls` | no `.Certificate` in the plan | serves cleartext |
//! | `requestLimit` | `-1` | answers until the listener is closed |
//! | `idleTimeoutMillis` | `-1` | waits for a connection indefinitely |
//! | `drainMillis` | no `.DrainMillis` in the plan | waits ten seconds for the requests in flight |
//!
//! The third column is what *this* runtime does, and it is written down so a
//! reader knows what to expect rather than so a program may rely on it. The
//! second column is the contract.
//!
//! **`protocols` and `tls` cross as one list**, which `effect Listen` calls the
//! plan and `bind` builds. That is worth knowing here for one reason: it is why
//! there is room for a knob at all. `listenBind` spends the ten integer
//! arguments a runtime call has, and a list crosses as a pointer and a length
//! however many things are in it — so the fields a `Server` grows from now on
//! are variants of `Serve` rather than arguments nobody has room for.
//!
//! **How many handlers run at once is still not on this list**, and it is not a
//! `Server` field at all — not for want of room now, but because it is an
//! *answer* rather than a setting. The acceptor says how many it will host, on
//! `Listener.handlers`, and `run` fans out to it. A program that has to bound
//! its own concurrency bounds it inside its handler.
//!
//! ## A handler runs on a task, and that is why `Tasks` is in the bound
//!
//! `run` does not drive one accept loop; it fans out `listener.handlers` of them
//! with `Tasks.parallel`, each on a carrier of its own, all accepting on the one
//! listener. A slow handler therefore holds up its own connection and nothing
//! else, which is what "many connections in flight" means here.
//!
//! **A worker pool and not a task per request**, and that is `effect Tasks`'s
//! shape showing through rather than a compromise. `parallel` is the whole of
//! that effect and it has no detached spawn: every task has finished before the
//! call returns, which is what keeps a task from outliving the context that
//! granted it. So there is no way to say "answer this one somewhere else and
//! come back for the next" — the loop that accepts is the loop that answers, and
//! running several of *those* is how they overlap. What a program gets is a
//! bounded concurrency it can read off `Listener.handlers`, which is a better
//! thing to have than an unbounded one: a server that starts a task per
//! connection is a server whose worst day is decided by its clients.
//!
//! `Tasks` in the bound is the visible price and it is the right one. Handlers
//! running at the same time is authority — the right to run a program's own work
//! concurrently — and this language puts authority in the context where a reader
//! looks for it. A program that grants `Listen` and not `Tasks` is not a slower
//! server; it does not compile, and the message names what is missing.
//!
//! ## TLS, and the protocol ALPN chooses
//!
//! A `Server` with a `tls` serves HTTPS, and the same handler answers both ways
//! — a handler never learns which transport its request arrived over, because
//! nothing about a `Request` depends on it.
//!
//! **HTTP/2 comes with TLS and only with TLS.** It is chosen inside the
//! handshake, by ALPN (RFC 7540 §3.3), so a `Server` asking for `.Http2`
//! without a certificate is `.Err(.Unsupported)` from `bind` with a message
//! saying so — refused rather than quietly answered in HTTP/1.1, because a
//! server that did not do what it was configured to do is the failure that is
//! found in production. A `Server` with a certificate and no `protocols` offers
//! HTTP/1.1: `.None` is the absence of a choice, and growing a certificate is
//! not a program asking for a second protocol.
//!
//! ```buri
//! from "core/effect" import { Alloc, Listen, Tasks };
//! from "core/host" import * as host;
//! from "core/net/http" import * as http;
//! from "core/net/server" import * as server;
//!
//! export fn main(): Result<(), Str> {
//!     let ctx = context {
//!         Alloc: host.alloc,
//!         Listen: host.listen,
//!         Tasks: host.tasks,
//!     };
//!     server
//!         .serve(ctx, server.Server {
//!             port: 8443,
//!             protocols: .Some([.Http1, .Http2]),
//!             tls: .Some(server.Tls {
//!                 certificate: "/etc/ssl/example.pem",
//!                 key: "/etc/ssl/example.key",
//!             }),
//!             onRequest: fn(c, _request) => http.text(c, "hello"),
//!         })
//!         .mapErr(server.errorText)
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Stopping, and what `.Ok(())` means
//!
//! `serve` answers `.Ok(())` when the server stopped **the way it was asked
//! to**, and there are three ways to ask. Two of them are fields: a
//! `requestLimit` that is spent, and a listener the program closed. The third
//! is the operating system's — a `SIGTERM` from a supervisor, or a `SIGINT`
//! from somebody at a terminal — and on a platform that grants `Listen` it does
//! not kill the process.
//!
//! What it does instead is a **drain**, in three steps:
//!
//! 1. the platform stops accepting connections;
//! 2. the requests already in flight are answered, and the workers holding them
//!    finish normally;
//! 3. `listenAccept` answers `.Closed`, every worker ends, the port is given
//!    back, and `serve` returns `.Ok(())`.
//!
//! So a `main` that ends with `serve(…)` falls off its own end when it is asked
//! to stop, which means whatever a program does after `serve` — flush a log,
//! close a database, print a line — happens. That is the whole point of
//! catching the signal rather than dying under it.
//!
//! `drainMillis` is how long step 2 may take. It bounds the *waiting* and not
//! the handlers: a handler still running when it expires still answers the
//! request it was given, and what expires is the patience of the workers with
//! nothing left to do. A client that opened a connection and sent nothing is
//! the case it exists for.
//!
//! **A second signal is not a second drain.** The platform gives the signal
//! back to the operating system when it catches the first one, so a second
//! `Ctrl-C` during a slow shutdown terminates the process the ordinary way —
//! which is what pressing it twice means everywhere else, and what stands
//! between a handler that will not return and a process that cannot be stopped.
//!
//! And a program that holds no port is not affected at all: the signals are the
//! platform's only while a listener is open, so a program that is not a server
//! keeps the operating system's own behaviour.
//!
//! ## What is here, and what is not yet
//!
//! One request per HTTP/1.1 connection: the response says `connection: close`
//! and the socket is dropped after it. Several requests share an HTTP/2
//! connection, which is what multiplexing is, and a worker answers each of them
//! exactly as it answers an HTTP/1.1 one. Keep-alive — several requests on one
//! *HTTP/1.1* connection — is still not here.
//!
//! `sendText`, `sendBytes` and `close` are the socket half. Nothing hands out
//! a `Socket` yet — a WebSocket upgrade is not something `serve` performs —
//! so the three exist because an effect method no standard-library function
//! calls is a method nothing outside `core/*` can perform (SPEC 10.2), and
//! they say what they will do rather than pretending to do it today.

from "core/effect" import {
    Alloc, Listen, Listener, Protocol, Request, Response, Serve, ServeError,
    ServeFailure, Sockets, Tasks,
};
from "core/list" import * as list;
from "core/tasks" import * as tasks;

from "core/effect" export {
    Listener, Protocol, Request, Response, Serve, ServeError, ServeFailure,
};

/// A server, whole.
///
/// `port` and `onRequest` are the two a server cannot be without: a port to
/// hold and something to answer with. Everything else is an `Option` that may
/// be left out of the literal, and `.None` means "the runtime chooses" — see
/// the module header, which is where that convention is argued rather than
/// merely stated.
///
/// `C` is the caller's own context, and it is what `onRequest` is handed. A
/// handler may therefore do anything the context it was written beside can do:
/// allocate a response body, print a line, read a clock, start a task. Nothing
/// about that authority passes through `Listen` — `run`'s workers call the
/// handler themselves — which is the reason the effect has five small methods
/// and not one big one.
export struct Server<C> {
    /// The port to hold. `0` asks the operating system to choose one, and
    /// `bind` answers with the number it chose.
    export port: Int,
    /// What answers a request. Called once per request, on one of `run`'s
    /// worker tasks, with the context `serve` was given.
    export onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
    /// The interface to listen on. `.None` lets the runtime choose, which on
    /// every platform that grants `Listen` today means loopback — a server
    /// that wants to be reachable from another machine says so.
    export address: Option<Str>,
    /// The HTTP versions to speak. `.None` is HTTP/1.1. A protocol this
    /// toolchain's runtime was not built with is `.Err(.Unsupported)` from
    /// `bind`, with a message naming the switch that would add it, and so is
    /// `.Http2` without a `tls` — see that field.
    export protocols: Option<[Protocol]>,
    /// Stop after answering this many requests, and return `.Ok(())`. `.None`
    /// serves until the listener is closed.
    ///
    /// It is a knob rather than a debugging aid: a server that is supposed to
    /// answer one request and exit is a real shape — a one-shot callback
    /// receiver, an OAuth redirect catcher, a test — and having it means a
    /// server can be *finished* by something other than being killed.
    export requestLimit: Option<Int>,
    /// Give up waiting for a connection after this long, and return
    /// `.Err(.Timeout)`. `.None` waits indefinitely, which is what an ordinary
    /// server does.
    export idleTimeoutMillis: Option<Int>,
    /// How long a shutdown may wait for the requests already in flight
    /// before it stops waiting. `.None` leaves the number to the runtime.
    ///
    /// It is the knob a deployment sets, because a deployment is the thing that
    /// decides how long a process has to stop — a supervisor that sends
    /// `SIGTERM` and then `SIGKILL` ten seconds later is asking a question this
    /// field answers. The module header is where the three steps of a drain are
    /// written out; the short version is that this bounds the middle one.
    export drainMillis: Option<Int>,
    /// The certificate this server presents, and therefore whether it speaks
    /// TLS at all. `.None` is a cleartext server.
    ///
    /// It is also what decides whether `.Http2` is reachable: HTTP/2 is chosen
    /// inside the TLS handshake (RFC 7540 §3.3), so a server that asks for it
    /// without a certificate is refused at the bind rather than quietly served
    /// in HTTP/1.1.
    export tls: Option<Tls>,
}

derive Eq, Show for Tls;
/// Where a server's identity is read from.
///
/// **Two paths and not two PEM documents**, which is a shape decision worth the
/// sentence. A certificate is a file that expires and gets rotated, and a
/// private key written into a source file is a private key in a version control
/// system — so what a program names is where to read them, and the reading is
/// the runtime's. They are read once, when the port is opened: a certificate
/// that is missing, unreadable, or does not match its key is an `.Err` from
/// `bind` and not a handshake failure at three in the morning.
///
/// Both fields are required, because half an identity is not one.
export struct Tls {
    /// The PEM file holding the certificate chain, leaf first.
    export certificate: Str,
    /// The PEM file holding the private key that goes with the leaf. PKCS#8,
    /// PKCS#1 and SEC1 are all read; the label in the file is what says which.
    export key: Str,
}

//
// Two knobs a reader might look for are still absent: **how long one connection
// may take to send its head**, and **the largest body the acceptor will read**.
// Both exist and both are the runtime's own numbers. They used to be absent
// because `listenBind` had no argument left to carry them; that is no longer
// true — the plan is a list and a list holds as many options as anyone writes —
// so they are absent now for the ordinary reason, which is that nobody has
// needed to name them yet. The day somebody does, they are two variants of
// `Serve` and two fields here.

/// The whole of a server: bind the port, answer requests until the listener is
/// done, give the port back.
///
/// `.Ok(())` means the server stopped the way it was asked to — its request
/// limit spent, its listener closed, or the platform asked to stop and finished
/// draining. An error is a reason it could not go on, and the port is given
/// back on that path too. The module header's "Stopping" section is where the
/// third of those is written out.
///
/// A program that needs the port *while* the server runs — anything binding
/// `port: 0` — wants `bind` and `run` instead, which are this function's two
/// halves and are exported for exactly that.
export fn serve<C: Alloc + Listen + Tasks>(
    ctx: C,
    server: Server<C>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (bind(ctx, server)) {
        .Err(e) => .Err(e),
        .Ok(listener) => run(ctx, listener, server),
    }
}

/// Opens the port and answers with it, without serving anything yet.
///
/// The point of the split is `port: 0`: the operating system chooses, and a
/// program that has to publish the number — write it to a file, print it, hand
/// it to a task that will connect — reads `listener.port` here, between the
/// bind and the loop.
/// **`Alloc` is in the bound because the plan is a list.** `listenBind` takes
/// one `[Serve]` — the shape `effect Listen` argues for, and the reason a
/// certificate could be added to a call that was already at its argument
/// budget — and building a list is an allocation, which this language says in
/// the bound rather than in a comment. `serve` already carried `Alloc` for
/// `run`'s fan-out, so nothing that called `serve` changes.
export fn bind<C: Alloc + Listen>(
    ctx: C,
    server: Server<C>,
): Result<Listener, ServeError> {
    ctx.listenBind(
        server.address.withDefault(""),
        server.port,
        planned(ctx, server),
        server.requestLimit.withDefault(-1),
        server.idleTimeoutMillis.withDefault(-1),
    )
}

/// A `Server`'s protocol and TLS fields, rendered into the one list the bind
/// takes.
///
/// The order is protocols first, then the identity, then the shutdown, which
/// is the order they are read in: the acceptor walks the protocols to decide
/// what it may offer, reads the certificate to decide whether it can offer
/// anything at all, and asks about the drain only when it is being asked to
/// stop. An empty list is a caller who chose nothing, and `.None` on every
/// field is exactly that.
///
/// **An absent option is an absent element**, which is what makes this the
/// `.None` convention rather than an encoding of it: there is no "unset"
/// `.DrainMillis` to render, because a plan that does not mention the drain is
/// already the sentence "this program did not choose one".
fn planned<C: Alloc>(ctx: C, server: Server<C>): [Serve] {
    let spoken: [Serve] = server.protocols.withDefault([]).map(ctx, fn(p) => .Speak(p));
    let secured: [Serve] = match (server.tls) {
        .None => spoken,
        .Some(tls) => {
            spoken.concat(ctx, [.Certificate(tls.certificate), .PrivateKey(tls.key)])
        },
    };
    match (server.drainMillis) {
        .None => secured,
        .Some(millis) => secured.concat(ctx, [.DrainMillis(millis)]),
    }
}

/// The most workers this module will start, whatever a `Listener` says.
///
/// A `Listener` is inert data and a program may write one for itself, so `run`
/// believes its `handlers` only inside a range it can host: a fan-out of a
/// million would be a program that stops making progress rather than a fast one,
/// and the number would have come from the program rather than from an acceptor.
/// The runtime's own ceiling is the same sixty-four (`cli/runtime/net.rs`, where
/// the reason is that a worker waiting for a connection holds a carrier), and
/// the two agreeing is a fact about this toolchain rather than a promise — the
/// clamp is here because this module cannot check what minted the value.
let MAX_HANDLERS: Int = 64;

/// `asked`, believed inside the range above. At least one: a server with no
/// workers is a port nobody is listening on.
fn hosted(asked: Int): Int {
    if (asked < 1) {
        1
    } else {
        if (asked > MAX_HANDLERS) { MAX_HANDLERS } else { asked }
    }
}

/// One worker's whole job: which listener to answer on, and what to answer
/// with.
///
/// It exists so that the fan-out's closure captures **nothing**. A task is
/// handed its item and its context and takes nothing from the frame it was
/// written in (SPEC 10.6), so the two things a worker needs travel as the item
/// rather than as a capture — which is also why `run` can hand every worker the
/// same value and let the acceptor decide who gets which request.
struct Worker<C> {
    handle: Int,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
}

/// Answers requests on an already-open listener until it is done, then closes
/// it.
///
/// **`listener.handlers` workers, each running the whole accept loop.** They all
/// accept on the one listener and the acceptor hands each connection to
/// whichever asks first, so a handler that takes a second holds up its own
/// connection and no other. The loop itself is `answering` below and is a tail
/// call: there are no loops (SPEC 15.1) and `middle/tail_calls.rs` rewrites a
/// self tail call into one, so each worker costs one frame however long it runs.
///
/// **One handler is no fan-out at all**, and that is worth the branch: an
/// acceptor that hosts one — every test double in this repository, and the
/// development backend's own answer — gets the loop on the calling task, with no
/// task started, no list built and no result folded. It is the server this
/// module was before F3, reached by asking rather than by a flag.
///
/// It takes the whole `Server` rather than its `onRequest` so that the two halves
/// of `serve` are the same value in and the same value out; `port`, `address` and
/// the bind knobs were spent by `bind` and are ignored here.
///
/// The answer is the **first** failure any worker reported, and `.Ok(())` when
/// every one of them ended the ordinary way. A worker that fails closes the
/// listener on its way out (see `answering`), so the others are not left waiting
/// out a server that has already stopped.
export fn run<C: Alloc + Listen + Tasks>(
    ctx: C,
    listener: Listener,
    server: Server<C>,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    let outcome = if (hosted(listener.handlers) <= 1) {
        answering(ctx, listener.handle, server.onRequest)
    } else {
        together(ctx, listener, server.onRequest)
    };
    let _closed = ctx.listenClose(listener.handle);
    outcome
}

/// The workers, fanned out, and the first failure any of them reported.
fn together<C: Alloc + Listen + Tasks>(
    ctx: C,
    listener: Listener,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    let worker = Worker { handle: listener.handle, onRequest: onRequest };
    let outcomes = tasks.parallel(
        ctx,
        list.repeat(ctx, worker, hosted(listener.handlers)),
        fn(c, _index, w) => answering(c, w.handle, w.onRequest),
    );
    outcomes.fold(
        fn(sofar, outcome) => {
            match (sofar) {
                .Err(e) => .Err(e),
                .Ok(_ok) => outcome,
            }
        },
        .Ok(()),
    )
}

/// One request, then the rest of them.
///
/// Four calls per turn — accept a connection, read its request, answer it, go
/// round. The first two are two rather than one because only the first of them
/// waits: `effect Listen` argues it where they are declared.
///
/// `.Closed` is the ordinary end and becomes `.Ok(())` here — the one place
/// that translation happens, so that no caller of this module ever has to know
/// a listener signs off by failing.
///
/// It is read from **either** call, and that is a deliberate widening rather
/// than a convenience. An acceptor may notice it is done when it takes the
/// last response as easily as when it is next asked for a request, and which
/// of the two it chooses is a fact about the acceptor. Reading only one would
/// make the other spelling a failure a server would have to know about.
fn answering<C: Listen>(
    ctx: C,
    handle: Int,
    onRequest: fn(C, Request) => Response,
): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (ctx.listenAccept(handle)) {
        .Err(e) => ending(ctx, handle, e),
        .Ok(connection) => {
            match (ctx.listenRequest(connection)) {
                .Err(e) => ending(ctx, handle, e),
                .Ok(request) => {
                    let handler = onRequest;
                    let reply = handler(ctx, request);
                    let answered = ctx.listenRespond(
                        connection,
                        reply.status,
                        reply.headers,
                        reply.body,
                    );
                    match (answered) {
                        .Err(e) => ending(ctx, handle, e),
                        .Ok(_ok) => answering(ctx, handle, onRequest),
                    }
                },
            }
        },
    }
}

/// How a worker ends: the ordinary way, or by taking the listener down with it.
///
/// `.Closed` is the ordinary end and becomes `.Ok(())`. Anything else is a
/// reason this worker cannot go on, and **the listener is closed before it is
/// reported** — because the other workers are blocked in an accept that nothing
/// else would interrupt, and a server whose first worker failed at nine in the
/// morning should not still be holding a port at noon. Closing is idempotent, so
/// every worker doing it is one close and n - 1 no-ops.
fn ending<C: Listen>(ctx: C, handle: Int, e: ServeError): Result<(), ServeError> {
    match (e.cause) {
        .Closed => .Ok(()),
        _ => {
            let _closed = ctx.listenClose(handle);
            .Err(e)
        },
    }
}

/// A `ServeError`'s cause as one line of English, for a `main` that answers
/// `Result<(), Str>`.
///
/// A free function rather than a method, for `http.errorText`'s reason:
/// `ServeError` is declared in `core/effect`, and a method must live in its
/// type's defining module.
///
/// The **cause only**, and it costs no context: it is a constant per variant,
/// so this needs no `Alloc` and can be used from anywhere, including the
/// `.mapErr` of a `main` whose context grants nothing but `Listen`. The
/// platform's own words are `e.detail`, and a program that wants both writes
/// `"${server.errorText(e)}: ${e.detail}"` — one interpolation, at the place
/// that already knows whether it has somewhere to print it.
export fn errorText(e: ServeError): Str {
    match (e.cause) {
        .AddressInUse => "the address is already in use",
        .AddressNotAvailable => "the address is not available on this machine",
        .PermissionDenied => "listening was not permitted",
        .Unsupported => "the protocol is not supported by this toolchain",
        .Timeout => "no connection arrived before the deadline",
        .Closed => "the listener is closed",
        .Transport => "the transport failed",
    }
}

// --- Sockets ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The other half of being a server, and the half with nothing behind it yet:
// `serve` performs no WebSocket upgrade, so nothing in this language hands out
// a socket. These three exist because an effect method no standard-library
// function calls is a method nothing outside `core/*` can perform — an effect
// is reached by handing a context to a function (SPEC 10.2), and these are
// those functions — and because the authority is granted with `Listen` rather
// than after it.
//
// A handle that names no open socket is treated as one that has already gone,
// which is the behaviour `Sockets` declares for a closed socket anyway. The
// `Socket` value with its own methods, `CloseReason` instead of a wire code,
// and the upgrade that mints one, land together.

/// Enqueues a text frame on an open socket. Never waits: the frame goes to the
/// socket's outbound buffer, and a socket whose buffer is full is closed
/// rather than its writer blocked.
export fn sendText<C: Sockets>(ctx: C, socket: Int, text: Str): () {
    ctx.socketSendText(socket, text)
}

/// Enqueues a binary frame, on `sendText`'s terms.
export fn sendBytes<C: Sockets>(ctx: C, socket: Int, body: [U8]): () {
    ctx.socketSendBytes(socket, body)
}

/// Closes the socket, with the wire code and reason the far side is told.
///
/// A message sent afterwards is dropped rather than refused: the other end may
/// already be gone, so "did this arrive" was never answerable here.
export fn close<C: Sockets>(ctx: C, socket: Int, code: Int, reason: Str): () {
    ctx.socketClose(socket, code, reason)
}
